\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Models.}
We probe three instruction-tuned models with comparable parameter counts but distinct architectures: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (32 layers, hidden dimension 4096) \citep{grattafiori2024llama}, Qwen3-8B (36 layers, hidden dimension 4096) \citep{yang2025qwen3}, and Gemma-2-9B-IT (42 layers, hidden dimension 3584) \citep{team2024gemma}.

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We use ConflictQA \citep{xie2024adaptive}, a benchmark of context-parametric knowledge conflicts where a provided context contradicts the model's parametric knowledge.
After verifying that each model can correctly answer the parametric-knowledge question without context, we retain 1{,}368 samples for Llama (1{,}096 train / 272 test), 966 for Qwen (773 train / 193 test), and 1{,}590 for Gemma (1{,}274 train / 316 test), split 80/20 with stratification.
The three models were independently verified, yielding only 3.5\% three-way question overlap (23 questions); pairwise overlaps range from 7.2\% (Llama $\cap$ Gemma) to 24.8\% (Gemma $\cap$ Qwen), so the consistent asymmetry pattern across models constitutes largely independent replication.

\paragraph{Translation.}
We translate all English contexts and questions into Chinese using a Qwen3-8B translation pipeline (EN$\to$ZH), with quality validated by COMET (wmt22-comet-da; \citealt{rei2020comet}) back-translation scores.

\paragraph{Probing.}
For each layer $l$, we train an $L_2$-regularized logistic regression probe ($C = 1.0$, scikit-learn default) on the last-token residual stream representation $\mathbf{h}_l$, with binary labels indicating whether the model chose the contextual or parametric answer.
We report balanced accuracy rather than AUROC, as our claims depend on cross-model directional comparisons; AUROC results are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:auroc}.
All CIs are computed via bootstrap resampling with paired differences for asymmetry estimation.
A selectivity control \citep{hewitt2019designing} with permuted labels on matched-condition data ($N{=}131$--$201$) yields near-chance accuracy (49.2--51.0\%) across all models, confirming selectivity gaps of 21--40~pp.
All experiments used a single GPU; the boundary replication (Appendix~\ref{app:boundary}) used an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000.

\paragraph{Non-conflict baseline.}
To test whether cross-lingual transfer patterns are specific to knowledge conflicts, we run the same probing pipeline on XNLI \citep{conneau2018xnli}, classifying entailment versus contradiction.

\subsection{Within-Language Conflict Detection}
\label{sec:within_lang}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports balanced accuracy for all probe transfer directions.
All three models encode linearly detectable conflict signals in their residual streams.
Llama achieves 77.6\% on EN$\to$EN (peak at layer 15, 47\% depth) and 72.1\% on ZH$\to$ZH.
Qwen reaches 75.0\% on EN$\to$EN (peak at layer 24, 67\% depth) and 72.6\% on ZH$\to$ZH.
Gemma attains 76.2\% on EN$\to$EN (peak at layer 24, 57\% depth) and 71.0\% on ZH$\to$ZH.
All within-language accuracies are significantly above chance (binomial $p < 0.001$).
Figure~\ref{fig:layer_probe} shows the layer-wise probe accuracy curves for all three models.
These results confirm that knowledge conflict signals are linearly separable in the residual stream across all three architectures and both languages.

%% Figure 2: Layer-wise probe accuracy
